   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.3. Increased capacity of Member States to design and deliver innovative, flexible and inclusive learning options, encompassing work-based learning and quality apprenticeships</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The government initiated a process of establishing regulatory framework on apprenticeships with a motivation to prevent exploitation of youth under the guise of training. Yet, it did not consult with social partners in a sufficient manner, and it most probably lacked understanding of diverse practices of work-based learning (WBL) on the ground. For instance, the proposed regulation obliges employers to pay the minimum wages to interns, to which the employers&#x2019; organization (ASK) opposed.

As a result of ILO&#x2019;s technical advice, the Ministry recognised differences of various types of WBL (e.g., internships of university students, practical training at work of vocational school students and trainees of public vocational training centres, practical training for occupational licences). It re-considers the proposed amendment to the Labour Code to clarify the scope of application of the regulation of apprenticeships and establish appropriate regulations for each type of WBL. The updated regulations of WBL are to be formulated.
 The ILO, with RBSA funding, advised the Ministry of Labour and Social Protection of Population on an appropriate regulatory framework for apprenticeship and internships through technical meetings and written recommendations. The ILO explained issues with proposed amendments to the Labour Code on apprenticeships. The bill did not distinguish various forms of work-based learning (WBL) such as internships, apprenticeships, and informal apprenticeships. In addition, the proposed amendments required employers to issue fix-term employment contract and pay the minimum wage payment to apprentices, which met with fierce opposition of employers. 

The ILO involved worker&#x2019;s and employer&#x2019;s organizations in discussions on the regulatory framework of apprenticeships and internships through social dialogue. Employers&#x2019; organization particularly appreciated ILO&#x2019;s advisory role and expressed its desire to further collaborate with the ILO on WBL.

The RBSA programme also designed and carried out field research in three regions of Azerbaijan to gather information on practices of WBL. The results showed divers practices of WBL and indicated a large room for improvement of skills training at workplaces. A lack of understanding of WBL among local stakeholders was evident. The Ministry requested ILO&#x2019;s continued engagement and assistance beyond the RBSA programme.</narrative>
   </description>
